  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_CHEMISTRY_SBLOCKELEMENTS_MCQ_Q01 - Chain structure and coordination of beryllium chloride</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>The polymeric chain structure of beryllium chloride (\(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\)) in the solid state is shown below:</p>
      <p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/img-1.jpeg" alt="Polymeric chain structure of solid beryllium chloride" /></p>
      <p>Based on this structure and the properties of \(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\), what is the coordination number of each \(\mathrm{Be}\) atom in the solid state, and what species does \(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\) predominantly form in the vapour phase at temperatures below \(1200\text{ K}\)?</p>
    ]]></text>
      <file name="img-1.jpeg" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The diagram shows the polymeric chain structure of solid beryllium chloride (\(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\)), in which each \(\mathrm{Be}\) atom is linked to adjacent \(\mathrm{Be}\) atoms through bridging chlorine (\(\mathrm{Cl}\)) atoms.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>Beryllium has only four valence orbitals (\(2s\) and three \(2p\)), so its maximum coordination number is \(4\). In the solid state, \(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\) achieves stability by forming a polymeric chain with bridging \(\mathrm{Cl}\) atoms.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Analysis of Solid and Vapour Phases</strong><br/>In the solid-state chain shown, each \(\mathrm{Be}\) atom is bonded to four \(\mathrm{Cl}\) atoms (two pairs of bridging \(\mathrm{Cl}\) atoms), giving a coordination number of \(4\). In the vapour phase, \(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\) tends to form a chloro-bridged dimer (\(\mathrm{Be}_2\mathrm{Cl}_4\)), which dissociates into a linear monomer only at high temperatures of the order of \(1200\text{ K}\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Final Conclusion</strong><br/>The coordination number of \(\mathrm{Be}\) in solid \(\mathrm{BeCl}_2\) is \(4\), and in the vapour phase below \(1200\text{ K}\) it tends to form a chloro-bridged dimer.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Coordination number \(2\); linear monomer</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Coordination number \(4\); chloro-bridged dimer</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Coordination number \(6\); octahedral polymer</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Coordination number \(4\); ionic crystal lattice</p>]]></text></answer>
