    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Given Data and Conditions</strong><br/>The graph plots the densities of the five non-radioactive Group 1 alkali metals (\(P, Q, R, S, T\)) in increasing order of atomic number: \(P = 0.53\text{ g cm}^{-3}\), \(Q = 0.97\text{ g cm}^{-3}\), \(R = 0.86\text{ g cm}^{-3}\), \(S = 1.53\text{ g cm}^{-3}\), and \(T = 1.90\text{ g cm}^{-3}\).</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Conceptual Principle</strong><br/>In Group 1, the non-radioactive alkali metals in order of increasing atomic number are \(\mathrm{Li}\) (\(Z=3\)), \(\mathrm{Na}\) (\(Z=11\)), \(\mathrm{K}\) (\(Z=19\)), \(\mathrm{Rb}\) (\(Z=37\)), and \(\mathrm{Cs}\) (\(Z=55\)). Density generally increases down the group, except that potassium is lighter than sodium.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Step-by-Step Identification</strong><br/>Matching the order of atomic numbers: \(P\) is \(\mathrm{Li}\) (\(0.53\text{ g cm}^{-3}\)), \(Q\) is \(\mathrm{Na}\) (\(0.97\text{ g cm}^{-3}\)), \(R\) is \(\mathrm{K}\) (\(0.86\text{ g cm}^{-3}\)), \(S\) is \(\mathrm{Rb}\) (\(1.53\text{ g cm}^{-3}\)), and \(T\) is \(\mathrm{Cs}\) (\(1.90\text{ g cm}^{-3}\)).<br/>In an oxidizing flame, potassium (\(\mathrm{K}\)) emits radiation in the visible region at \(\lambda = 766.5\text{ nm}\), which corresponds to a violet colour.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Check and Final Answer</strong><br/>Lithium gives crimson red, sodium gives yellow, rubidium gives red violet, and caesium gives blue. Thus, element \(R\) is Potassium (\(\mathrm{K}\)) and it imparts a Violet colour to an oxidizing flame.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
